        year = 2026,
        summary = "A design in which every worker may organise and bargain under a legal right that a neutral public body enforces. Councils elected by all staff cover each site, sector-wide bargaining sets the baseline, and no state may ban strikes, give one union exclusive rights, or change bargaining law without review.",
        details = "Keeps: a legal right to organise and bargain, enforced by a federal agency, from Keynesian welfare capitalism (US National Labor Relations (Wagner) Act); councils elected by all staff, union or not, from Social market economy (German works councils and the Works Constitution Act); sector-wide bargaining, from Nordic social democracy (Nordic bargaining coverage and falling union density). Blocks: strike bans and exclusive representation for one regime-aligned confederation, from Corporatism and fascist economics (Italy's 1926 Rocco law on strikes and unions); safeguard: strikes and rival unions protected by the courts. Blocks: falling density after reforms, from Nordic social democracy; safeguard: any change to bargaining law tested against coverage figures first. Test: union density, bargaining coverage and the share of workers with a council seat, measured each year for ten years. Source: Macmillan-hosted textbook chapter; Max Planck Society legal-history article; Fafo paper (2022); Trinity College Dublin document.",
